\ String literals are constructed directly at their stack slot in AOT mode:
\ the Value's tag, slice pointer, and length are written via ValueLayout
\ offsets, with the slice pointing at the binary's static const char[]. No
\ jitPushString trampoline crossing and no per-literal allocation. Correct
\ output proves the directly-constructed Value behaves identically to the
\ interpreter across printing, concatenation, length, slicing, the empty
\ string, and a literal carrying an embedded quote and escape.
"hello" print-line
"hello" " " #append "world" #append print-line
"test" #len .
"abcdef" 1 3 #slice print-line
"" #len .
"" print-line
"a\"b\\c" print-line
